It makes sense that CS doesn’t ask for photos of the vehicle being roadworthy, considering these are fleet vehicles. However, if we don’t ask for photos of the vehicle damages, it could make it more difficult understanding whether it’s cosmetic damage or structural damage. As you know, we shouldn’t block for cosmetic damages, but we should still inform the vehicle owner of the issue. 

As I see it, there are two options, please see them below in bold:

*Option 1:*

* *Do not* block the driver;
* Let the driver know that his vehicle will be blocked;
* *Ask for photos of the vehicle damages, but do not ask the driver for photos of the vehicle being roadworthy nor proof of repair;*
* *Instruct the driver to contact the fleet owner regarding the vehicle block;*
** *The Operations team will send a link requesting the photos to the fleet owner, and only after sending the photos via the link will the car be unlocked.*
* Local Ops will be automatically notified of the report;
* Local Ops will block the vehicle and follow up when needed;
* Close the investigation *without blockings*, unless otherwise specified by the Local Team.

*Option 2:*

* *Do not* block the driver;
* Let the driver know that his vehicle will be blocked;
* *Do not ask the driver for photos of the vehicle being roadworthy/proof of repair nor the vehicle damages;*
* *Instruct the driver to contact the fleet owner regarding the vehicle block;*
** *The Operations team will send a link requesting the photos to the fleet owner, and only after sending the photos via the link will the car be unlocked.*
* Local Ops will be automatically notified of the report;
* Local Ops will block the vehicle and follow up when needed;
* Close the investigation *without blockings*, unless otherwise specified by the Local Team.



Please let me know which option best describes your market’s needs, without compromising the investigation into the issue. 🙂

