{quote}* TypeScript doesn't support tag/marker interfaces, because it has a structural type system. It means that when we have {{interface A {x:number;} interface B {x: number;}}}, variables {{const a: A = {x: 1}; let b: B = {x: 2};}} are compatible in terms of type, i.e. you can write {{b = a;}}, because the structures of the types are compatible. In comparison, the "normal" type system is called "nominative", like in Java, i.e. names of types/interfaces do matter, and you cannot assign {{a}} to {{b}}, because {{A}} is not {{B}}. In TypeScript only structures of types matter, thus talking about *marker interfaces* is confusing - all *the explanations should be fixed*. But that interfaces are useful, as they give names to useful things and structurally the types will differ due to the different set of options for the type of {{type}}, and... see the next point.{quote}

This is a very interesting discussion about structural and nominative type system. 
