Hey [~accountid:5cfe57d9a7095c0c3ffac38d],

the safety escalation guidelines were revamped some time ago ([thread here|https://taxify.slack.com/archives/C01S7AVSJ3W/p1693471461472489]), and the decision to ask the user to submit a new ticket for the non-safety issue was made. 

I agree that it makes no sense and contributes to a bad user experience. 

As I see it, the current process states the following: 

{{Case includes a non-safety issue as well as a safety issue}}

{{Safety case includes a non-safety question or issue that is completely unrelated to the safety part (e.g. rider inquires about areas of operation while raising a complaint about the driver's violent behaviour during the ride; driver complains about a violent rider and simultaneously talks about low overall earnings):}}

* {{Advise the user to raise a separate ticket for the non-safety issue}}
* {{Remove non-safety issue tags added by the user}}

{{Safety case includes a non-safety question or issue that is related to the safety part (anything related to the same journey, such as overcharge on top of a safety event):}}

* {{If equipped to deal with the issue yourself, handle the non-safety part once the safety investigation is concluded}}
* {{If not equipped to deal with such an issue, deescalate to Tier 1 (keeping the severity level) once you conclude the safety investigation}}

In my opinion, to make the user experience better, we could remove the first part ({{Safety case includes a non-safety question or issue that is completely unrelated to the safety part }}), and regardless of whether the non-safety issue is related to the safety part or not, the agent solves the safety part first and then deals with the non-safety issue if equipped to deal with it or de-escalates it to Tier 1. But I don’t know how this would work from data perspective, e.g. case tagging, severity level, tier, etc. 

Recently, this question was raised again [here|https://taxify.atlassian.net/browse/CCM-15340], but no one from CS Safety or Risk & Safety contributed when asked repeatedly. 

Either way, this decision is on CS Safety, so there are 2 options:

